---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Layout title="Retro Web Design" filename="articles/retro.htm">
  <p><a href={`${base}articles/`} class="back-link">← Back to Articles</a></p>

  <h1>Bringing Back Retro Web Design</h1>

  <p><em>Published: January 10, 2024</em></p>

  <hr />

  <p>
    The early web was a wild place. Animated GIFs, tiled backgrounds, visitor counters,
    and "Best viewed in Netscape Navigator" badges. It was chaotic, beautiful, and
    deeply personal.
  </p>

  <h2>The Aesthetic Elements</h2>

  <p>If you want to capture that retro vibe, here are the key visual elements:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Beveled buttons</strong> — That classic Windows 95/98 look</li>
    <li><strong>System fonts</strong> — Tahoma, MS Sans Serif, Comic Sans MS</li>
    <li><strong>Table layouts</strong> — Everything in tables!</li>
    <li><strong>Under construction GIFs</strong> — The site is never finished</li>
    <li><strong>Visitor counters</strong> — Gotta track those hits</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Modern Tools for Retro Feels</h2>

  <p>
    You don't need FrontPage anymore to create these experiences. Modern CSS gives us
    all the power we need to recreate those classic UI elements.
  </p>

  <h2>Finding the Balance</h2>

  <p>
    The challenge is honoring the aesthetic without the usability nightmares. We can have
    retro visuals while still being responsive and accessible.
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal is to capture the <em>feeling</em> of those websites — the sense of
    discovery, the personal touch, the creativity.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <div class="fp-nav-bar">
    <a href={`${base}articles/`} class="fp-nav-btn">◀ Back to Articles</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
